<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cumbria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cumbria is a county of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, created in 1974 mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> and Westmorland. It includes the Lake District and the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was part of British kingdoms such as Rheged and Strathclyde, whose Brittonic speech gave the region its name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cumbria.jpg" alt="Cumbria" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derwent_Water,_Lake_District,_Cumbria_-_June_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other parts of the island kept Celtic languages: Gaelic, and perhaps some Pictish, in most of Scotland, Cornish in Cornwall and parts of Devon, Cumbric perhaps to the 12th century in parts of <b>Cumbria</b>, and Welsh in Wales.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Malcolm IV died unmarried at 24, after a reign in which he surrendered Northumbria and <b>Cumbria</b> to Henry II of England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carvetii lived in the Eden valley and around Carlisle in what is now <b>Cumbria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/carvetii">Carvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>Its location is uncertain, but scholars usually place it around Carlisle, <b>Cumbria</b> and Galloway.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rheged">Kingdom of Rheged</a></li><li><blockquote>The renewed kingdom, often called <b>Cumbria</b>, expanded south into Cumbria in the 10th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a></li></ul></section>
